syntax:
A constructor is a special member function with the same name as the class and no return type. It is called automatically when an object is created. Syntax example:
class ClassName {
public:
    ClassName(param1_type param1, param2_type param2) : member1(param1), member2(param2) {}  // Member initializer list for efficient initialization
private:
    member_type member1;
    member_type member2;
};
Practical usage note: Use the member initializer list for efficiency and to avoid default initialization followed by assignment. Always prefer constructors over direct member access for initialization to enforce invariants and improve code safety.

code_example:
#include <string>

class Sales_data {  // Simplified version inspired by C++ Primer's Sales_data
public:
    Sales_data(const std::string& bookNo, unsigned units_sold = 0, double revenue = 0.0)
        : bookNo_(bookNo), units_sold_(units_sold), revenue_(revenue) {}  // Constructor with initializer list

    // Other members like combine function can be added here

private:
    std::string bookNo_;  // ISBN
    unsigned units_sold_ = 0;  // Number of units sold
    double revenue_ = 0.0;  // Total revenue
};

int main() {
    Sales_data item("0-123-45678-9", 10, 19.99);  // Using constructor to initialize
    // This creates an object with proper initialization
    return 0;
}
